Field Value Severity CRITICAL CVSS 9.1 CWE CWE-613 (Insufficient Session Expiration) Location <code>api/util.py:38-47</code> OWASP A07:2021 - Identification and Authentication Failures <p>Evidence (<code>api/util.py</code> lines 38-47):</p> <pre><code>class AuthJWTManager(AbstractAuthTokenManager):
    def provide_token(self, user: 'AbstractBaseUser') -&gt; AuthToken:
        encoded_jwt = jwt.encode(
            {"user_id": user.pk},      # &lt;-- NO "exp" claim
            settings.SECRET_KEY,
            algorithm=JWTAuthentication.ALGORITHM,
        )
        return AuthToken(encoded_jwt)
</code></pre> <p>The <code>provide_token()</code> method generates JWTs with only <code>{"user_id": &lt;pk&gt;}</code> and no <code>exp</code> (expiration) claim. While <code>SIMPLE_JWT</code> settings configure <code>ACCESS_TOKEN_LIFETIME = 1 day</code>, the custom <code>AuthJWTManager</code> bypasses SimpleJWT entirely by using raw <code>jwt.encode()</code>.</p> <p>The <code>JWTAuthentication.authenticate()</code> method (line 70-96) only catches <code>jwt.ExpiredSignatureError</code> but never encounters it because no <code>exp</code> is set. These tokens are valid forever.</p> <p>Additionally, <code>revoke_token()</code> (line 49-51) raises <code>AuthTokenNotRevoked()</code>, meaning tokens cannot be invalidated even when a user changes their password or is deactivated.</p> <p>Impact: If any JWT token is leaked (logs, browser storage, network intercept), it provides permanent access to the associated account with no way to revoke it.</p> <p>Remediation:</p> <pre><code>def provide_token(self, user: 'AbstractBaseUser') -&gt; AuthToken:
    from datetime import datetime, timedelta, timezone
    encoded_jwt = jwt.encode(
        {
            "user_id": user.pk,
            "exp": datetime.now(timezone.utc) + timedelta(hours=1),
            "iat": datetime.now(timezone.utc),
        },
        settings.SECRET_KEY,
        algorithm=JWTAuthentication.ALGORITHM,
    )
    return AuthToken(encoded_jwt)
</code></pre> <p>Also implement token revocation via a blocklist or token versioning.</p>

Field Value Severity CRITICAL CVSS 7.5 CWE CWE-532 (Insertion of Sensitive Information into Log File) Location <code>api/views/views_auth.py:29</code> OWASP A09:2021 - Security Logging and Monitoring Failures <p>Evidence:</p> <pre><code># views_auth.py:29
logger.warning(f'Invalid change password request: {serializer.errors}, {request.data}')
</code></pre> <p>When a password change request fails validation, <code>request.data</code> is logged verbatim. This dict contains <code>old_password</code>, <code>password</code>, and potentially <code>password2</code> in plaintext. These values are written to: - Console output - Log files on disk - Sentry (if error propagates) - Admin email (via <code>mail_admins</code> handler)</p> <p>Additional instances of <code>request.data</code> being logged: - <code>views_trip.py:61</code> - trip creation data - <code>views_trip.py:131</code> - trip edit data - <code>views_upgraderequest.py:41</code> - upgrade request data - <code>views_upgraderequest.py:180</code> - upgrade request comment data</p> <p>Remediation:</p> <pre><code># Never log request.data directly - sanitize first
logger.warning(f'Invalid change password request: {serializer.errors}')
# Or create a sanitize function:
def sanitize_log_data(data):
    sensitive_keys = {'password', 'old_password', 'password1', 'password2', 'token'}
    return {k: '***' if k in sensitive_keys else v for k, v in data.items()}
</code></pre>

Location <code>ad4x4/settings.py</code> - REST_FRAMEWORK CWE CWE-307 (Improper Restriction of Excessive Authentication Attempts) <p>There are no <code>DEFAULT_THROTTLE_CLASSES</code> or <code>DEFAULT_THROTTLE_RATES</code> configured in DRF settings. While Nginx provides some rate limiting (200 req/min on <code>/api/</code>, 4 req/min on <code>/api/auth/register</code>), the following endpoints have no Django-level throttling:</p> <ul> <li><code>POST /api/token/</code> (JWT token obtain)</li> <li><code>POST /api/auth/login/</code> (login)</li> <li><code>POST /api/auth/send-reset-password-link/</code> (password reset)</li> <li><code>POST /api/auth/change-password/</code> (password change)</li> </ul> <p>Nginx rate limiting is also broken because Cloudflare <code>set_real_ip_from</code> directives are all commented out (see H13).</p> <p>Remediation:</p> <pre><code>REST_FRAMEWORK = {
    'DEFAULT_THROTTLE_CLASSES': [
        'rest_framework.throttling.AnonRateThrottle',
        'rest_framework.throttling.UserRateThrottle',
    ],
    'DEFAULT_THROTTLE_RATES': {
        'anon': '20/minute',
        'user': '60/minute',
    }
}
</code></pre>

Location <code>infra/nginx/secure/default.conf.template:6-30</code> <p>All Cloudflare <code>set_real_ip_from</code> directives and <code>real_ip_header CF-Connecting-IP</code> are commented out:</p> <pre><code>#set_real_ip_from 173.245.48.0/20;
...
#real_ip_header CF-Connecting-IP;
</code></pre> <p>Nginx rate limiting uses <code>$binary_remote_addr</code>, which behind Cloudflare resolves to Cloudflare's IP, not the client's. This means: - All users share the same rate limit bucket - Rate limiting is effectively disabled - A single user's traffic can exhaust the limit for all users</p> <p>Remediation: Uncomment the Cloudflare IP ranges and <code>real_ip_header CF-Connecting-IP</code>.</p>

Location <code>website/views.py:54-55</code> CWE CWE-601 (URL Redirection to Untrusted Site) OWASP A01:2021 - Broken Access Control <pre><code>next_url = request.GET.get('next', '/')
return redirect(next_url)
</code></pre> <p>The <code>next</code> parameter is taken directly from user input and used in a redirect without validation. An attacker can craft a URL like:</p> <pre><code>https://ap.ad4x4.com/login/?next=https://evil.com/phishing
</code></pre> <p>After a victim logs in, they are redirected to the attacker's site, which can mimic the AD4x4 site to steal credentials or session tokens.</p> <p>Remediation:</p> <pre><code>from django.utils.http import url_has_allowed_host_and_scheme

next_url = request.GET.get('next', '/')
if not url_has_allowed_host_and_scheme(next_url, allowed_hosts={request.get_host()}):
    next_url = '/'
return redirect(next_url)
</code></pre>

Location <code>website/views.py:526</code> CWE CWE-352 (Cross-Site Request Forgery) <pre><code>@csrf_exempt
def paypal_webhook(request):
</code></pre> <p>The webhook endpoint has CSRF protection disabled. While this is standard for webhook receivers, the compensating control (PayPal signature verification) is conditional — it only runs if <code>PAYPAL_WEBHOOK_ID</code> is configured (see M4). Without it, any actor can send forged POST requests to this endpoint.</p> <p>Combined with the lack of IP allowlisting (PayPal webhook source IPs are well-documented), this creates a viable attack vector.</p> <p>Remediation: Make webhook signature verification mandatory (not optional). Add PayPal IP allowlisting as defense in depth.</p>

Location <code>infra/nginx/secure/default.conf.template</code> <p>Current security headers:</p> <pre><code>add_header X-Frame-Options "DENY";
add_header X-Content-Type-Options "nosniff";
add_header Referrer-Policy "no-referrer-when-downgrade";
</code></pre> <p>Missing: - <code>Content-Security-Policy</code> — prevents XSS, clickjacking, data injection - <code>Strict-Transport-Security</code> — forces HTTPS - <code>Permissions-Policy</code> — controls browser features</p>

Location Multiple views <pre><code># views_firebase.py:52
return Response({'detail': f'Firebase error: {e}'}, status=500)

# views_firebase.py:58
return Response({'detail': f'An unexpected error occurred: {e}'}, status=500)
</code></pre> <p>Internal error messages, stack traces, and exception details are returned to the client, potentially revealing: - Internal architecture (Firebase, Redis, PostgreSQL) - File paths and package versions - SQL query structures</p>

Location <code>api/views/views_configurablesettings.py:33</code> <pre><code>def get_permissions(self):
    return [AllowAny()]  # For read (list/retrieve)
</code></pre> <p>Configurable settings (potentially including internal business logic parameters) are publicly readable.</p>

Location <code>ad4x4/settings.py:422</code> <pre><code>SIMPLE_JWT = {
    "ACCESS_TOKEN_LIFETIME": timedelta(days=1),
}
</code></pre> <p>Industry standard is 15-60 minutes for access tokens with a separate refresh token mechanism. A 24-hour token window increases the risk if a token is compromised.</p> <p>Note: This is moot for the custom JWT (C2) which has NO expiration, but applies to any tokens generated via the SimpleJWT <code>TokenObtainPairView</code>.</p>
